{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack devtools allows you to create your own custom plugins by emitting and listening to our event bus."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"prerequisite","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Prerequisite"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This guide will walk you through a simple example where our library is a counter with a count history. A working example can be found in our "},{"type":"link","href":"https://tanstack.com/devtools/latest/docs/framework/preact/examples/custom-devtools","children":[{"type":"text","value":"custom-plugin example"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This is our library code:"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"counter.ts"}]},{"type":"code","value":"export function createCounter() {\n  let count = 0\n  const history = []\n\n  return {\n    getCount: () => count,\n    increment: () => {\n      count++\n      history.push(count)\n    },\n    decrement: () => {\n      count--\n      history.push(count)\n    },\n  };\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"event-client-setup","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Event Client Setup"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Install the "},{"type":"link","href":"https://www.npmjs.com/package/@tanstack/devtools-event-client","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Devtools Event Client"}]},{"type":"text","value":" utils."}]},{"type":"code","value":"npm i @tanstack/devtools-event-client","lang":"bash"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"First you will need to setup the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"EventClient"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"eventClient.ts"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { EventClient } from '@tanstack/devtools-event-client'\n\n\ntype EventMap = {\n  // The key is the event suffix only — the pluginId is prepended automatically by EventClient\n  // The value is the expected type of the event payload\n  'counter-state': { count: number, history: number[] }\n}\n\nclass CustomEventClient extends EventClient<EventMap> {\n  constructor() {\n    super({\n      // The pluginId is prepended to event map keys when emitting/listening\n      pluginId: 'custom-devtools',\n    })\n  }\n}\n\n// This is where the magic happens, it'll be used throughout your application.\nexport const DevtoolsEventClient = new CustomEventClient()","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"event-client-integration","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Event Client Integration"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Now we need to hook our "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"EventClient"},{"type":"text","value":" into the application code. This can be done in many way's, a useEffect that emits the current state, or a subscription to an observer, all that matters is that when you want to emit the current state you do the following."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Our new library code will looks as follows:"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"counter.ts"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { DevtoolsEventClient } from './eventClient.ts'\n\nexport function createCounter() {\n  let count = 0\n  const history: Array<number> = []\n\n  return {\n    getCount: () => count,\n    increment: () => {\n      count++\n      history.push(count)\n\n      // The emit eventSuffix must match that of the EventMap defined in eventClient.ts\n      DevtoolsEventClient.emit('counter-state', {\n        count,\n        history,\n      })\n    },\n    decrement: () => {\n      count--\n      history.push(count)\n\n      DevtoolsEventClient.emit('counter-state', {\n        count,\n        history,\n      })\n    },\n  }\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"callout","kind":"important","title":"Important","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"EventClient"},{"type":"text","value":" is framework agnostic so this process will be the same regardless of framework or even in vanilla JavaScript."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"consuming-the-event-client","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Consuming The Event Client"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Now we need to create our devtools panel, for a simple approach write the devtools in the framework that the adapter is, be aware that this will make the plugin framework specific."}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Because TanStack is framework agnostic we have taken a more complicated approach that will be explained in coming docs (if framework agnosticism is not a concern to you, you can ignore this)."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"DevtoolsPanel.tsx"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { useEffect, useState } from 'preact/hooks'\nimport { DevtoolsEventClient } from './eventClient.ts'\n\nexport function DevtoolPanel() {\n  const [state, setState] = useState<{ count: number; history: number[] } | undefined>()\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    // subscribe to the emitted event\n    const cleanup = DevtoolsEventClient.on(\"counter-state\", e => setState(e.payload))\n    return cleanup\n  }, [])\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <div>{state?.count}</div>\n      <div>{JSON.stringify(state?.history)}</div>\n    </div>\n  )\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"application-integration","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Application Integration"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This step follows what's shown in "},{"type":"link","href":"../basic-setup","children":[{"type":"text","value":"basic-setup"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for a more documented guide go check it out. As well as the complete "},{"type":"link","href":"https://tanstack.com/devtools/latest/docs/framework/preact/examples/custom-devtools","children":[{"type":"text","value":"custom-devtools example"}]},{"type":"text","value":" in our examples section."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Main.tsx"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { render } from 'preact'\nimport { DevtoolPanel } from './DevtoolPanel'\n\nrender(\n  <>\n    <App />\n\n    <TanStackDevtools\n      plugins={[\n        {\n          // Name it what you like, this is how it will appear in the Menu\n          name: 'Custom devtools',\n          render: <DevtoolPanel />,\n        },\n      ]}\n    />\n  </>,\n  document.getElementById('root')!,\n)\n","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"debugging","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Debugging"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Both the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TanStackDevtools"},{"type":"text","value":" component and the TanStack "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"EventClient"},{"type":"text","value":" come with built in debug mode which will log to the console the emitted event as well as the EventClient status."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStackDevtool's debugging mode can be activated like so:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"<TanStackDevtools\n  eventBusConfig={{ debug: true }}\n  plugins={[\n    {\n      // call it what you like, this is how it will appear in the Menu\n      name: 'Custom devtools',\n      render: <DevtoolPanel />,\n    },\n  ]}\n/>","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Where as the EventClient's debug mode can be activated by:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"class CustomEventClient extends EventClient<EventMap> {\n  constructor() {\n    super({\n      pluginId: 'custom-devtools',\n      debug: true,\n    })\n  }\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Activating the debug mode will log to the console the current events that emitter has emitted or listened to. The EventClient will have appended "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"[tanstack-devtools:${pluginId}]"},{"type":"text","value":" and the client will have appended "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"[tanstack-devtools:client-bus]"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Heres an example of both:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"🌴 [tanstack-devtools:client-bus] Initializing client event bus\n\n🌴 [tanstack-devtools:custom-devtools-plugin] Registered event to bus custom-devtools:counter-state"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"production-builds","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Production builds"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"By default the "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"root import"}]},{"type":"text","value":" of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/devtools-event-client"},{"type":"text","value":" no-ops\noutside development. When your bundler sets "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"process.env.NODE_ENV"},{"type":"text","value":" to anything\nother than "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'development'"},{"type":"text","value":", the real client is replaced by a no-op and\ntree-shaken out of your production bundle:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// dev: real client — production: no-op (tree-shaken away)\nimport { EventClient } from '@tanstack/devtools-event-client'","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you are an open-source author who deliberately wants devtools events in\nproduction, import the real client from the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/production"},{"type":"text","value":" subpath instead. It is\nnever stripped:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// always the real client, in every environment\nimport { EventClient } from '@tanstack/devtools-event-client/production'","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The public API is identical between the two imports — only the production\nruntime behavior differs."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"prerequisite","text":"Prerequisite","level":2},{"id":"event-client-setup","text":"Event Client Setup","level":2},{"id":"event-client-integration","text":"Event Client Integration","level":2},{"id":"consuming-the-event-client","text":"Consuming The Event Client","level":2},{"id":"application-integration","text":"Application Integration","level":2},{"id":"debugging","text":"Debugging","level":2},{"id":"production-builds","text":"Production builds","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nTanStack devtools allows you to create your own custom plugins by emitting and listening to our event bus.\n\n## Prerequisite\n\nThis guide will walk you through a simple example where our library is a counter with a count history. A working example can be found in our [custom-plugin example](https://tanstack.com/devtools/latest/docs/framework/preact/examples/custom-devtools).\n\nThis is our library code:\n\ncounter.ts\n```tsx\nexport function createCounter() {\n  let count = 0\n  const history = []\n\n  return {\n    getCount: () => count,\n    increment: () => {\n      count++\n      history.push(count)\n    },\n    decrement: () => {\n      count--\n      history.push(count)\n    },\n  };\n}\n```\n\n## Event Client Setup\n\nInstall the [TanStack Devtools Event Client](https://www.npmjs.com/package/@tanstack/devtools-event-client) utils.\n\n```bash\nnpm i @tanstack/devtools-event-client\n```\n\nFirst you will need to setup the `EventClient`.\n\neventClient.ts\n```tsx\nimport { EventClient } from '@tanstack/devtools-event-client'\n\n\ntype EventMap = {\n  // The key is the event suffix only — the pluginId is prepended automatically by EventClient\n  // The value is the expected type of the event payload\n  'counter-state': { count: number, history: number[] }\n}\n\nclass CustomEventClient extends EventClient<EventMap> {\n  constructor() {\n    super({\n      // The pluginId is prepended to event map keys when emitting/listening\n      pluginId: 'custom-devtools',\n    })\n  }\n}\n\n// This is where the magic happens, it'll be used throughout your application.\nexport const DevtoolsEventClient = new CustomEventClient()\n```\n\n## Event Client Integration\n\nNow we need to hook our `EventClient` into the application code. This can be done in many way's, a useEffect that emits the current state, or a subscription to an observer, all that matters is that when you want to emit the current state you do the following.\n\nOur new library code will looks as follows:\n\ncounter.ts\n```tsx\nimport { DevtoolsEventClient } from './eventClient.ts'\n\nexport function createCounter() {\n  let count = 0\n  const history: Array<number> = []\n\n  return {\n    getCount: () => count,\n    increment: () => {\n      count++\n      history.push(count)\n\n      // The emit eventSuffix must match that of the EventMap defined in eventClient.ts\n      DevtoolsEventClient.emit('counter-state', {\n        count,\n        history,\n      })\n    },\n    decrement: () => {\n      count--\n      history.push(count)\n\n      DevtoolsEventClient.emit('counter-state', {\n        count,\n        history,\n      })\n    },\n  }\n}\n```\n\n> [!IMPORTANT] \n> `EventClient` is framework agnostic so this process will be the same regardless of framework or even in vanilla JavaScript.\n\n## Consuming The Event Client\n\nNow we need to create our devtools panel, for a simple approach write the devtools in the framework that the adapter is, be aware that this will make the plugin framework specific.\n\n> Because TanStack is framework agnostic we have taken a more complicated approach that will be explained in coming docs (if framework agnosticism is not a concern to you, you can ignore this).\n\nDevtoolsPanel.tsx\n```tsx\nimport { useEffect, useState } from 'preact/hooks'\nimport { DevtoolsEventClient } from './eventClient.ts'\n\nexport function DevtoolPanel() {\n  const [state, setState] = useState<{ count: number; history: number[] } | undefined>()\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    // subscribe to the emitted event\n    const cleanup = DevtoolsEventClient.on(\"counter-state\", e => setState(e.payload))\n    return cleanup\n  }, [])\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <div>{state?.count}</div>\n      <div>{JSON.stringify(state?.history)}</div>\n    </div>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n## Application Integration\n\nThis step follows what's shown in [basic-setup](../basic-setup) for a more documented guide go check it out. As well as the complete [custom-devtools example](https://tanstack.com/devtools/latest/docs/framework/preact/examples/custom-devtools) in our examples section.\n\nMain.tsx\n```tsx\nimport { render } from 'preact'\nimport { DevtoolPanel } from './DevtoolPanel'\n\nrender(\n  <>\n    <App />\n\n    <TanStackDevtools\n      plugins={[\n        {\n          // Name it what you like, this is how it will appear in the Menu\n          name: 'Custom devtools',\n          render: <DevtoolPanel />,\n        },\n      ]}\n    />\n  </>,\n  document.getElementById('root')!,\n)\n\n```\n\n## Debugging\n\nBoth the `TanStackDevtools` component and the TanStack `EventClient` come with built in debug mode which will log to the console the emitted event as well as the EventClient status.\n\nTanStackDevtool's debugging mode can be activated like so:\n```tsx\n<TanStackDevtools\n  eventBusConfig={{ debug: true }}\n  plugins={[\n    {\n      // call it what you like, this is how it will appear in the Menu\n      name: 'Custom devtools',\n      render: <DevtoolPanel />,\n    },\n  ]}\n/>\n```\n\nWhere as the EventClient's debug mode can be activated by:\n```tsx\nclass CustomEventClient extends EventClient<EventMap> {\n  constructor() {\n    super({\n      pluginId: 'custom-devtools',\n      debug: true,\n    })\n  }\n}\n```\n\nActivating the debug mode will log to the console the current events that emitter has emitted or listened to. The EventClient will have appended `[tanstack-devtools:${pluginId}]` and the client will have appended `[tanstack-devtools:client-bus]`.\n\nHeres an example of both:\n```\n🌴 [tanstack-devtools:client-bus] Initializing client event bus\n\n🌴 [tanstack-devtools:custom-devtools-plugin] Registered event to bus custom-devtools:counter-state\n```\n\n## Production builds\n\nBy default the **root import** of `@tanstack/devtools-event-client` no-ops\noutside development. When your bundler sets `process.env.NODE_ENV` to anything\nother than `'development'`, the real client is replaced by a no-op and\ntree-shaken out of your production bundle:\n\n```ts\n// dev: real client — production: no-op (tree-shaken away)\nimport { EventClient } from '@tanstack/devtools-event-client'\n```\n\nIf you are an open-source author who deliberately wants devtools events in\nproduction, import the real client from the `/production` subpath instead. It is\nnever stripped:\n\n```ts\n// always the real client, in every environment\nimport { EventClient } from '@tanstack/devtools-event-client/production'\n```\n\nThe public API is identical between the two imports — only the production\nruntime behavior differs.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Custom plugins\nid: custom-plugins\n---\n\nTanStack devtools allows you to create your own custom plugins by emitting and listening to our event bus.\n\n## Prerequisite\n\nThis guide will walk you through a simple example where our library is a counter with a count history. A working example can be found in our [custom-plugin example](https://tanstack.com/devtools/latest/docs/framework/preact/examples/custom-devtools).\n\nThis is our library code:\n\ncounter.ts\n```tsx\nexport function createCounter() {\n  let count = 0\n  const history = []\n\n  return {\n    getCount: () => count,\n    increment: () => {\n      count++\n      history.push(count)\n    },\n    decrement: () => {\n      count--\n      history.push(count)\n    },\n  };\n}\n```\n\n## Event Client Setup\n\nInstall the [TanStack Devtools Event Client](https://www.npmjs.com/package/@tanstack/devtools-event-client) utils.\n\n```bash\nnpm i @tanstack/devtools-event-client\n```\n\nFirst you will need to setup the `EventClient`.\n\neventClient.ts\n```tsx\nimport { EventClient } from '@tanstack/devtools-event-client'\n\n\ntype EventMap = {\n  // The key is the event suffix only — the pluginId is prepended automatically by EventClient\n  // The value is the expected type of the event payload\n  'counter-state': { count: number, history: number[] }\n}\n\nclass CustomEventClient extends EventClient<EventMap> {\n  constructor() {\n    super({\n      // The pluginId is prepended to event map keys when emitting/listening\n      pluginId: 'custom-devtools',\n    })\n  }\n}\n\n// This is where the magic happens, it'll be used throughout your application.\nexport const DevtoolsEventClient = new CustomEventClient()\n```\n\n## Event Client Integration\n\nNow we need to hook our `EventClient` into the application code. This can be done in many way's, a useEffect that emits the current state, or a subscription to an observer, all that matters is that when you want to emit the current state you do the following.\n\nOur new library code will looks as follows:\n\ncounter.ts\n```tsx\nimport { DevtoolsEventClient } from './eventClient.ts'\n\nexport function createCounter() {\n  let count = 0\n  const history: Array<number> = []\n\n  return {\n    getCount: () => count,\n    increment: () => {\n      count++\n      history.push(count)\n\n      // The emit eventSuffix must match that of the EventMap defined in eventClient.ts\n      DevtoolsEventClient.emit('counter-state', {\n        count,\n        history,\n      })\n    },\n    decrement: () => {\n      count--\n      history.push(count)\n\n      DevtoolsEventClient.emit('counter-state', {\n        count,\n        history,\n      })\n    },\n  }\n}\n```\n\n> [!IMPORTANT] \n> `EventClient` is framework agnostic so this process will be the same regardless of framework or even in vanilla JavaScript.\n\n## Consuming The Event Client\n\nNow we need to create our devtools panel, for a simple approach write the devtools in the framework that the adapter is, be aware that this will make the plugin framework specific.\n\n> Because TanStack is framework agnostic we have taken a more complicated approach that will be explained in coming docs (if framework agnosticism is not a concern to you, you can ignore this).\n\nDevtoolsPanel.tsx\n```tsx\nimport { useEffect, useState } from 'preact/hooks'\nimport { DevtoolsEventClient } from './eventClient.ts'\n\nexport function DevtoolPanel() {\n  const [state, setState] = useState<{ count: number; history: number[] } | undefined>()\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    // subscribe to the emitted event\n    const cleanup = DevtoolsEventClient.on(\"counter-state\", e => setState(e.payload))\n    return cleanup\n  }, [])\n\n  return (\n    <div>\n      <div>{state?.count}</div>\n      <div>{JSON.stringify(state?.history)}</div>\n    </div>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n## Application Integration\n\nThis step follows what's shown in [basic-setup](../basic-setup) for a more documented guide go check it out. As well as the complete [custom-devtools example](https://tanstack.com/devtools/latest/docs/framework/preact/examples/custom-devtools) in our examples section.\n\nMain.tsx\n```tsx\nimport { render } from 'preact'\nimport { DevtoolPanel } from './DevtoolPanel'\n\nrender(\n  <>\n    <App />\n\n    <TanStackDevtools\n      plugins={[\n        {\n          // Name it what you like, this is how it will appear in the Menu\n          name: 'Custom devtools',\n          render: <DevtoolPanel />,\n        },\n      ]}\n    />\n  </>,\n  document.getElementById('root')!,\n)\n\n```\n\n## Debugging\n\nBoth the `TanStackDevtools` component and the TanStack `EventClient` come with built in debug mode which will log to the console the emitted event as well as the EventClient status.\n\nTanStackDevtool's debugging mode can be activated like so:\n```tsx\n<TanStackDevtools\n  eventBusConfig={{ debug: true }}\n  plugins={[\n    {\n      // call it what you like, this is how it will appear in the Menu\n      name: 'Custom devtools',\n      render: <DevtoolPanel />,\n    },\n  ]}\n/>\n```\n\nWhere as the EventClient's debug mode can be activated by:\n```tsx\nclass CustomEventClient extends EventClient<EventMap> {\n  constructor() {\n    super({\n      pluginId: 'custom-devtools',\n      debug: true,\n    })\n  }\n}\n```\n\nActivating the debug mode will log to the console the current events that emitter has emitted or listened to. The EventClient will have appended `[tanstack-devtools:${pluginId}]` and the client will have appended `[tanstack-devtools:client-bus]`.\n\nHeres an example of both:\n```\n🌴 [tanstack-devtools:client-bus] Initializing client event bus\n\n🌴 [tanstack-devtools:custom-devtools-plugin] Registered event to bus custom-devtools:counter-state\n```\n\n## Production builds\n\nBy default the **root import** of `@tanstack/devtools-event-client` no-ops\noutside development. When your bundler sets `process.env.NODE_ENV` to anything\nother than `'development'`, the real client is replaced by a no-op and\ntree-shaken out of your production bundle:\n\n```ts\n// dev: real client — production: no-op (tree-shaken away)\nimport { EventClient } from '@tanstack/devtools-event-client'\n```\n\nIf you are an open-source author who deliberately wants devtools events in\nproduction, import the real client from the `/production` subpath instead. It is\nnever stripped:\n\n```ts\n// always the real client, in every environment\nimport { EventClient } from '@tanstack/devtools-event-client/production'\n```\n\nThe public API is identical between the two imports — only the production\nruntime behavior differs.\n"}
